\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Structure of the study. We keep one evaluation harness fixed and change four axes on top of it. The harness scores every kernel in an isolated subprocess. This is required, not optional, because one bad kernel can corrupt the CUDA context, and then every later evaluation in the same process fails. We do not propose a new generation system. We hold the harness constant and change one axis at a time, so that we can tell apart the effect of the model, the hardware, the benchmark, and the compute budget.}}{4}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{4}{Structure of the study. We keep one evaluation harness fixed and change four axes on top of it. The harness scores every kernel in an isolated subprocess. This is required, not optional, because one bad kernel can corrupt the CUDA context, and then every later evaluation in the same process fails. We do not propose a new generation system. We hold the harness constant and change one axis at a time, so that we can tell apart the effect of the model, the hardware, the benchmark, and the compute budget}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@LN@col{1}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Accuracy on all 100 tasks of KernelBench Level~1 under the small budget. Every row uses the same harness and the same task set. The AutoTriton row is the mean and standard deviation over four independent runs; every other row is a single run.}}{4}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
